en: '<p class="l-text"><strong>Every real program must interact with the outside world.</strong> It reads configuration from files, writes results to disk, fetches data from APIs, and sometimes things go wrong — files disappear, networks timeout, users provide garbage input. This lesson covers two pillars of production Python: <strong>File I/O</strong> (getting data in and out) and <strong>Error Handling</strong> (gracefully surviving the unexpected). Master these, and you can build software that runs reliably in the messy real world.</p>'

+ '<p class="l-text">We will start with the fundamentals of reading and writing files, then level up to context managers, structured data formats (JSON, CSV), robust exception handling, professional logging, and filesystem operations with both <code>os</code> and the modern <code>pathlib</code>. Every section includes practical patterns you will use in real projects — from data science pipelines to web scrapers to ML training scripts.</p>'
+ '<div class="lesson-outcomes" style="background:rgba(200,169,110,0.06);border-left:3px solid #c8a96e;padding:1rem 1.2rem;margin:1.5rem 0;border-radius:0 8px 8px 0"><div style="font-size:0.78rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.08em;color:#c8a96e;margin-bottom:0.6rem">📍 WHAT YOU\'LL LEARN</div><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.3rem;line-height:1.65;font-size:0.93rem;color:rgba(235,230,220,0.92)"><li>Read and write text, JSON, and CSV files using built-in modules</li><li>Manage paths and directories portably with pathlib</li><li>Parse command-line arguments with argparse for real CLI tools</li><li>Use the logging module instead of print for production diagnostics</li><li>Handle resources safely with with-statements and context managers</li></ul></div>'


/* ============================================================
   SECTION 1: READING & WRITING FILES
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">1. Reading & Writing Files</h2>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>The open() function</strong> is your gateway to the filesystem. It returns a <em>file object</em> (also called a file handle or stream) that provides methods for reading and writing. Every file you open must eventually be closed — but as we will see in Section 2, Python has elegant ways to handle this automatically.</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>File modes</strong> control what you can do with a file. Each mode is a short string passed to <code>open()</code>:</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">r</div><div class="cc-title">Read (default)</div><div class="cc-body">Read text. File must exist.<br><code>open("f.txt", "r")</code></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">w</div><div class="cc-title">Write</div><div class="cc-body">Write text. Creates or <strong>truncates</strong> file.<br><code>open("f.txt", "w")</code></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">a</div><div class="cc-title">Append</div><div class="cc-body">Write to end. Creates if missing.<br><code>open("f.txt", "a")</code></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">x</div><div class="cc-title">Exclusive Create</div><div class="cc-body">Create new file. Fails if exists.<br><code>open("f.txt", "x")</code></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">rb</div><div class="cc-title">Read Binary</div><div class="cc-body">Read raw bytes (images, PDFs).<br><code>open("img.png", "rb")</code></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">wb</div><div class="cc-title">Write Binary</div><div class="cc-body">Write raw bytes to file.<br><code>open("out.bin", "wb")</code></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">r+</div><div class="cc-title">Read + Write</div><div class="cc-body">Both read and write. File must exist.<br><code>open("f.txt", "r+")</code></div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-icon">t</div><div class="cc-title">Text (default)</div><div class="cc-body">Text mode, handles encoding.<br>Implicit in "r", "w", "a".</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Reading files</strong> — Python provides three approaches depending on how much data you need:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PSEUDOCODE</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># 1. read() — load entire file into a single string</span>\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"poem.txt"</span>, <span class="str">"r"</span>, encoding=<span class="str">"utf-8"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n    content = f.<span class="fn">read</span>()       <span class="cm"># entire file as one string</span>\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(content)\n\n<span class="cm"># 2. readline() — read one line at a time</span>\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"data.txt"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n    first_line = f.<span class="fn">readline</span>()   <span class="cm"># includes \\n at end</span>\n    second_line = f.<span class="fn">readline</span>()\n\n<span class="cm"># 3. readlines() — list of all lines</span>\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"data.txt"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n    lines = f.<span class="fn">readlines</span>()     <span class="cm"># ["line1\\n", "line2\\n", ...]</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># 4. Best practice: iterate directly (memory-efficient)</span>\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"big_file.txt"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n    <span class="kw">for</span> line <span class="kw">in</span> f:            <span class="cm"># reads line by line, low memory</span>\n        line = line.<span class="fn">strip</span>()   <span class="cm"># remove \\n and whitespace</span>\n        <span class="kw">if</span> line:\n            <span class="fn">process</span>(line)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Encoding matters!</strong> Always specify <code>encoding="utf-8"</code> when working with text files, especially on Windows where the default might be <code>cp1252</code>. This prevents garbled characters in files containing non-ASCII text (Turkish, Chinese, emoji, etc.).</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Writing files</strong> — use <code>write()</code> for strings and <code>writelines()</code> for iterables:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># Writing text</span>\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"output.txt"</span>, <span class="str">"w"</span>, encoding=<span class="str">"utf-8"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n    f.<span class="fn">write</span>(<span class="str">"Hello, World!\\n"</span>)          <span class="cm"># write() does NOT add \\n</span>\n    f.<span class="fn">write</span>(<span class="str">"Second line\\n"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># writelines() — writes an iterable of strings (no newlines added)</span>\nlines = [<span class="str">"alpha\\n"</span>, <span class="str">"beta\\n"</span>, <span class="str">"gamma\\n"</span>]\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"output.txt"</span>, <span class="str">"w"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n    f.<span class="fn">writelines</span>(lines)\n\n<span class="cm"># Appending to existing file</span>\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"log.txt"</span>, <span class="str">"a"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n    f.<span class="fn">write</span>(<span class="str">"[2024-01-15] New entry\\n"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># print() can write to files too!</span>\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"report.txt"</span>, <span class="str">"w"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Score:"</span>, <span class="num">95.5</span>, file=f)  <span class="cm"># adds space + newline automatically</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>The modern approach — pathlib.Path:</strong> Since Python 3.4, the <code>pathlib</code> module provides an object-oriented interface to filesystem paths. It combines path manipulation <em>and</em> file I/O in one clean API:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">from</span> pathlib <span class="kw">import</span> Path\n\n<span class="cm"># Reading and writing in one line</span>\ncontent = Path(<span class="str">"config.txt"</span>).<span class="fn">read_text</span>(encoding=<span class="str">"utf-8"</span>)\nPath(<span class="str">"output.txt"</span>).<span class="fn">write_text</span>(<span class="str">"Hello!"</span>, encoding=<span class="str">"utf-8"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Binary files</span>\nimage_data = Path(<span class="str">"photo.jpg"</span>).<span class="fn">read_bytes</span>()\nPath(<span class="str">"copy.jpg"</span>).<span class="fn">write_bytes</span>(image_data)\n\n<span class="cm"># Path construction with / operator</span>\nbase = Path(<span class="str">"data"</span>) / <span class="str">"2024"</span> / <span class="str">"january"</span>\nbase.<span class="fn">mkdir</span>(parents=<span class="kw">True</span>, exist_ok=<span class="kw">True</span>)\nfile_path = base / <span class="str">"report.csv"</span>\nfile_path.<span class="fn">write_text</span>(<span class="str">"col1,col2\\n1,2\\n"</span>)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><strong>Think about it:</strong> Why does <code>write()</code> not add a newline but <code>print()</code> does? Because <code>write()</code> is a low-level method that puts exactly what you give it — no magic. <code>print()</code> is a high-level convenience function designed for human-readable output, so it adds separators and a trailing newline by default. In production code, you want the control that <code>write()</code> gives you.</div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 2: CONTEXT MANAGERS
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">2. Context Managers</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>The <code>with</code> statement</strong> is one of Python\'s most important features for writing reliable code. It guarantees that resources (files, database connections, locks, network sockets) are properly cleaned up, even when exceptions occur. Without it, a single crash can leave files corrupted, databases locked, or memory leaked.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-compare"><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">WITHOUT with (DANGEROUS)</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; Must remember to call close()</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; Exception before close() = resource leak</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; Requires try/finally boilerplate</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; Easy to forget, hard to debug</div></div><div class="compare-col"><div class="compare-title">WITH with (SAFE)</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; Automatic cleanup guaranteed</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; Exception-safe by design</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; Clean, readable syntax</div><div class="compare-item">&bull; Pythonic best practice</div></div></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PSEUDOCODE</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># ❌ The dangerous way — manual resource management</span>\nf = <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"data.txt"</span>)\n<span class="kw">try</span>:\n    content = f.<span class="fn">read</span>()\n    <span class="fn">process</span>(content)    <span class="cm"># if this crashes, finally still runs</span>\n<span class="kw">finally</span>:\n    f.<span class="fn">close</span>()            <span class="cm"># must always close!</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># ✅ The Pythonic way — with statement</span>\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"data.txt"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n    content = f.<span class="fn">read</span>()\n    <span class="fn">process</span>(content)\n<span class="cm"># f is automatically closed here, even if process() raises an exception</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Multiple context managers (Python 3.10+ parenthesized form)</span>\n<span class="kw">with</span> (\n    <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"input.txt"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> src,\n    <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"output.txt"</span>, <span class="str">"w"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> dst\n):\n    dst.<span class="fn">write</span>(src.<span class="fn">read</span>().<span class="fn">upper</span>())</code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>How context managers work under the hood:</strong> Any object with <code>__enter__</code> and <code>__exit__</code> methods can be used with <code>with</code>. When the <code>with</code> block starts, Python calls <code>__enter__()</code> and binds the return value to the <code>as</code> variable. When the block ends (normally or via exception), Python calls <code>__exit__()</code>.</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># Custom context manager — class-based</span>\n<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">Timer</span>:\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__enter__</span>(self):\n        <span class="kw">import</span> time\n        self.start = time.<span class="fn">perf_counter</span>()\n        <span class="kw">return</span> self\n\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__exit__</span>(self, exc_type, exc_val, exc_tb):\n        <span class="kw">import</span> time\n        self.elapsed = time.<span class="fn">perf_counter</span>() - self.start\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Elapsed: </span>{self.elapsed:<span class="num">.4</span>f}<span class="str">s"</span>)\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">False</span>   <span class="cm"># False = don\'t suppress exceptions</span>\n\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">Timer</span>() <span class="kw">as</span> t:\n    total = <span class="fn">sum</span>(<span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">10_000_000</span>))\n<span class="cm"># Prints: Elapsed: 0.2341s</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Simpler: using contextlib decorator</span>\n<span class="kw">from</span> contextlib <span class="kw">import</span> contextmanager\n\n@contextmanager\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">temp_directory</span>():\n    <span class="kw">import</span> tempfile, shutil\n    tmpdir = tempfile.<span class="fn">mkdtemp</span>()\n    <span class="kw">try</span>:\n        <span class="kw">yield</span> tmpdir        <span class="cm"># value bound to \'as\' variable</span>\n    <span class="kw">finally</span>:\n        shutil.<span class="fn">rmtree</span>(tmpdir)  <span class="cm"># cleanup always runs</span>\n\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">temp_directory</span>() <span class="kw">as</span> d:\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Working in </span>{d}<span class="str">"</span>)\n    <span class="cm"># do work in temp directory...</span>\n<span class="cm"># directory is deleted automatically</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>Common built-in context managers:</strong> <code>open()</code> for files, <code>threading.Lock()</code> for thread safety, <code>sqlite3.connect()</code> for databases, <code>decimal.localcontext()</code> for precision control, <code>tempfile.TemporaryFile()</code> for temp files, and <code>unittest.mock.patch()</code> for testing. You will encounter context managers everywhere in Python.</div>'

/* ============================================================
   SECTION 3: WORKING WITH DATA FORMATS
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">3. Working with Data Formats</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text">Raw text files are just the beginning. Real-world data comes in <strong>structured formats</strong> — JSON for APIs, CSV for spreadsheets and datasets, and occasionally binary formats like pickle. Python\'s standard library has dedicated modules for each, so you rarely need third-party packages for basic data I/O.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps">'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">JSON</div><div class="step-text"><strong>JavaScript Object Notation</strong> — The lingua franca of web APIs and configuration files. Human-readable, language-independent, maps naturally to Python dicts and lists.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">CSV</div><div class="step-text"><strong>Comma-Separated Values</strong> — The simplest tabular format. Used for datasets, spreadsheet exports, and data exchange. Each row is a line, fields separated by commas.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">PKL</div><div class="step-text"><strong>Pickle</strong> — Python\'s native serialization. Can store any Python object (classes, functions, numpy arrays). Fast but Python-only and has security risks.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON — JSON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> json\n\n<span class="cm"># Writing JSON</span>\ndata = {\n    <span class="str">"name"</span>: <span class="str">"Alice"</span>,\n    <span class="str">"scores"</span>: [<span class="num">95</span>, <span class="num">87</span>, <span class="num">92</span>],\n    <span class="str">"graduated"</span>: <span class="kw">False</span>,\n    <span class="str">"address"</span>: {<span class="str">"city"</span>: <span class="str">"Istanbul"</span>, <span class="str">"country"</span>: <span class="str">"Turkey"</span>}\n}\n\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"student.json"</span>, <span class="str">"w"</span>, encoding=<span class="str">"utf-8"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n    json.<span class="fn">dump</span>(data, f, indent=<span class="num">2</span>, ensure_ascii=<span class="kw">False</span>)  <span class="cm"># pretty + unicode</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Reading JSON</span>\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"student.json"</span>, <span class="str">"r"</span>, encoding=<span class="str">"utf-8"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n    loaded = json.<span class="fn">load</span>(f)     <span class="cm"># returns dict</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># JSON to/from strings (no file)</span>\njson_str = json.<span class="fn">dumps</span>(data, indent=<span class="num">2</span>)\nparsed = json.<span class="fn">loads</span>(json_str)\n\n<span class="cm"># Custom encoder for non-standard types</span>\n<span class="kw">from</span> datetime <span class="kw">import</span> datetime\n\n<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">DateEncoder</span>(json.JSONEncoder):\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">default</span>(self, obj):\n        <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="fn">isinstance</span>(obj, datetime):\n            <span class="kw">return</span> obj.<span class="fn">isoformat</span>()\n        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">super</span>().<span class="fn">default</span>(obj)\n\nrecord = {<span class="str">"event"</span>: <span class="str">"login"</span>, <span class="str">"time"</span>: <span class="fn">datetime</span>.<span class="fn">now</span>()}\njson.<span class="fn">dumps</span>(record, cls=DateEncoder)  <span class="cm"># works!</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON — CSV</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> csv\n\n<span class="cm"># Writing CSV</span>\nstudents = [\n    {<span class="str">"name"</span>: <span class="str">"Alice"</span>, <span class="str">"grade"</span>: <span class="str">"A"</span>, <span class="str">"score"</span>: <span class="num">95</span>},\n    {<span class="str">"name"</span>: <span class="str">"Bob"</span>, <span class="str">"grade"</span>: <span class="str">"B+"</span>, <span class="str">"score"</span>: <span class="num">87</span>},\n    {<span class="str">"name"</span>: <span class="str">"Charlie"</span>, <span class="str">"grade"</span>: <span class="str">"A-"</span>, <span class="str">"score"</span>: <span class="num">91</span>},\n]\n\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"grades.csv"</span>, <span class="str">"w"</span>, newline=<span class="str">""</span>, encoding=<span class="str">"utf-8"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n    writer = csv.<span class="fn">DictWriter</span>(f, fieldnames=[<span class="str">"name"</span>, <span class="str">"grade"</span>, <span class="str">"score"</span>])\n    writer.<span class="fn">writeheader</span>()\n    writer.<span class="fn">writerows</span>(students)\n\n<span class="cm"># Reading CSV with DictReader (header row becomes keys)</span>\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"grades.csv"</span>, <span class="str">"r"</span>, encoding=<span class="str">"utf-8"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n    reader = csv.<span class="fn">DictReader</span>(f)\n    <span class="kw">for</span> row <span class="kw">in</span> reader:\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f\'</span>{row[<span class="str">"name"</span>]}<span class="str">: </span>{row[<span class="str">"score"</span>]}<span class="str">\'</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Basic csv.reader — returns lists</span>\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"data.csv"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n    reader = csv.<span class="fn">reader</span>(f)\n    header = <span class="fn">next</span>(reader)     <span class="cm"># skip header row</span>\n    <span class="kw">for</span> row <span class="kw">in</span> reader:\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(row)             <span class="cm"># ["Alice", "A", "95"]</span></code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON — PICKLE</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> pickle\n\n<span class="cm"># Serialize any Python object to bytes</span>\nmodel = {<span class="str">"weights"</span>: [<span class="num">0.5</span>, <span class="num">-0.3</span>, <span class="num">0.8</span>], <span class="str">"bias"</span>: <span class="num">0.1</span>, <span class="str">"epochs"</span>: <span class="num">100</span>}\n\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"model.pkl"</span>, <span class="str">"wb"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n    pickle.<span class="fn">dump</span>(model, f)\n\n<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(<span class="str">"model.pkl"</span>, <span class="str">"rb"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n    loaded_model = pickle.<span class="fn">load</span>(f)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Security Warning:</strong> Never <code>pickle.load()</code> data from untrusted sources! Pickle can execute arbitrary code during deserialization. An attacker can craft a malicious pickle file that runs <code>os.system("rm -rf /")</code> when loaded. For untrusted data, use JSON or CSV instead. In ML, consider using <code>safetensors</code> or <code>torch.load(weights_only=True)</code>.</div>'

