/* ============================================================
   SECTION 4: EXCEPTION HANDLING
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">4. Exception Handling</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Exceptions are not bugs — they are a communication mechanism.</strong> When something unexpected happens, Python raises an exception: an object that describes what went wrong. Your job is to decide which exceptions you can handle gracefully (recover and continue) and which should propagate up (crash with a clear message). Good exception handling is the difference between a program that says "Error" and one that says "File not found: config.yaml — using default settings."</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight"><strong>The try/except/else/finally quartet:</strong> Python gives you four blocks for exception handling. <code>try</code> contains the risky code. <code>except</code> handles specific errors. <code>else</code> runs only if no exception occurred. <code>finally</code> always runs, for cleanup. Most developers forget <code>else</code> — but it is essential for keeping the "happy path" separate from error-handling code.</div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PSEUDOCODE</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">load_config</span>(path):\n    <span class="kw">try</span>:\n        <span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(path) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n            config = json.<span class="fn">load</span>(f)\n    <span class="kw">except</span> FileNotFoundError:\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Config not found: </span>{path}<span class="str">, using defaults"</span>)\n        config = {<span class="str">"debug"</span>: <span class="kw">False</span>, <span class="str">"port"</span>: <span class="num">8080</span>}\n    <span class="kw">except</span> json.JSONDecodeError <span class="kw">as</span> e:\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Invalid JSON in </span>{path}<span class="str">: </span>{e}<span class="str">"</span>)\n        config = {}\n    <span class="kw">except</span> PermissionError:\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"No permission to read </span>{path}<span class="str">"</span>)\n        <span class="kw">raise</span>   <span class="cm"># re-raise — this is unrecoverable</span>\n    <span class="kw">else</span>:\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Config loaded successfully from </span>{path}<span class="str">"</span>)\n    <span class="kw">finally</span>:\n        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Config loading attempt complete"</span>)\n    <span class="kw">return</span> config\n\n<span class="cm"># Catching multiple exception types</span>\n<span class="kw">try</span>:\n    value = <span class="fn">int</span>(user_input)\n<span class="kw">except</span> (ValueError, TypeError) <span class="kw">as</span> e:\n    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">f"Invalid input: </span>{e}<span class="str">"</span>)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps">'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-text"><strong>try</strong> — Contains code that might raise an exception. Keep this block as short as possible to avoid catching exceptions you did not intend to handle.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-text"><strong>except</strong> — Catches specific exception types. Always catch the most specific exception possible. Never use bare <code>except:</code> — it catches SystemExit and KeyboardInterrupt, which you almost never want.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-text"><strong>else</strong> — Runs only when try succeeds with no exceptions. Put your "happy path" code here so it does not accidentally trigger the except block.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="step-item"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-text"><strong>finally</strong> — Always runs, whether an exception occurred or not. Use for guaranteed cleanup: closing connections, releasing locks, deleting temp files.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Custom exceptions</strong> make your error messages domain-specific and your except blocks more precise:</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PSEUDOCODE</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="cm"># Custom exception hierarchy</span>\n<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">AppError</span>(Exception):\n    <span class="str">"""Base exception for our application."""</span>\n    <span class="kw">pass</span>\n\n<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">ValidationError</span>(AppError):\n    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(self, field, message):\n        self.field = field\n        self.message = message\n        <span class="fn">super</span>().<span class="fn">__init__</span>(<span class="str">f"</span>{field}<span class="str">: </span>{message}<span class="str">"</span>)\n\n<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">DatabaseError</span>(AppError):\n    <span class="kw">pass</span>\n\n<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">NotFoundError</span>(DatabaseError):\n    <span class="kw">pass</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Usage</span>\n<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">validate_age</span>(age):\n    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="kw">not</span> <span class="fn">isinstance</span>(age, <span class="fn">int</span>):\n        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">ValidationError</span>(<span class="str">"age"</span>, <span class="str">"must be an integer"</span>)\n    <span class="kw">if</span> age < <span class="num">0</span> <span class="kw">or</span> age > <span class="num">150</span>:\n        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">ValidationError</span>(<span class="str">"age"</span>, <span class="str">f"</span>{age}<span class="str"> is out of valid range (0-150)"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Exception chaining — preserve the original cause</span>\n<span class="kw">try</span>:\n    data = json.<span class="fn">loads</span>(raw_text)\n<span class="kw">except</span> json.JSONDecodeError <span class="kw">as</span> e:\n    <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">ValidationError</span>(<span class="str">"payload"</span>, <span class="str">"invalid JSON format"</span>) <span class="kw">from</span> e\n    <span class="cm"># The original JSONDecodeError is preserved in __cause__</span></code></pre></div>'

